### What is token-based authentication?

Token-based authentication is a stateless approach where the server issues a self-contained, signed token after successful login. The token carries the user's identity and claims inside it. The client stores the token and sends it on every request. The server validates the token by checking the signature and expiry without consulting any session store.

The most common token format is JWT (JSON Web Token), which has three Base64Url-encoded parts: Header, Payload, and Signature.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import io.jsonwebtoken.security.Keys;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import java.util.Date;

public class JwtTokenService {

    private static final SecretKey KEY = Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(
        "national-bank-secret-key-256-bits-long!!!".getBytes());
    private static final long EXPIRY_MS = 15 * 60 * 1000; // 15 minutes

    public String issueToken(String username, String role) {
        return Jwts.builder()
            .subject(username)
            .claim("role", role)
            .issuer("national-bank")
            .issuedAt(new Date())
            .expiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis() + EXPIRY_MS))
            .signWith(KEY)
            .compact();
    }
}
```

```text
Generated token for rajesh:
eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJyYWplc2giLCJyb2xlIjoiQ1VTVE9NRVIi...signature
```

### How does token validation work?

The server receives the token in the `Authorization: Bearer` header. It splits the token, recomputes the signature over the header and payload using the configured key, and compares it to the signature in the token. If they match, the token is genuine and unmodified. Then it checks the `exp` claim against the current time.

No database call is needed. This is what makes token-based auth truly stateless.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import io.jsonwebtoken.Claims;
import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import io.jsonwebtoken.security.Keys;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;

public class JwtValidator {

    private static final SecretKey KEY = Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(
        "national-bank-secret-key-256-bits-long!!!".getBytes());

    public Claims validate(String token) {
        return Jwts.parser()
            .verifyWith(KEY)
            .requireIssuer("national-bank")
            .build()
            .parseSignedClaims(token)
            .getPayload();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JwtTokenService service = new JwtTokenService();
        String token = service.issueToken("rajesh", "CUSTOMER");

        JwtValidator validator = new JwtValidator();
        Claims claims = validator.validate(token);

        System.out.println("Subject: " + claims.getSubject());
        System.out.println("Role: " + claims.get("role"));
        System.out.println("Issuer: " + claims.getIssuer());
        System.out.println("Expires: " + claims.getExpiration());
    }
}
```

```text
Subject: rajesh
Role: CUSTOMER
Issuer: national-bank
Expires: 2024-10-27T10:15:00Z
```

### Why is token-based auth better for microservices?

In a microservices architecture with an Account Service, Order Service, and Settlement Service, each service can independently verify the same token using a shared signing key. There is no central session store to become a bottleneck or single point of failure. Adding a new instance requires zero session replication.

With session-based auth, every instance would need access to the session store. With token-based auth, the signing key is all that is needed.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.jsonwebtoken.Claims;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestHeader;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
public class AccountServiceController {

    private final JwtValidator jwtValidator = new JwtValidator();

    @GetMapping("/api/v1/balance")
    public String getBalance(@RequestHeader("Authorization") String authHeader) {
        String token = authHeader.replace("Bearer ", "");
        Claims claims = jwtValidator.validate(token);
        String user = claims.getSubject();
        String role = claims.get("role", String.class);
        // No session lookup. No database call for authentication.
        return "Balance for " + user + " (" + role + "): "
            + new BigDecimal("125000.75") + " INR";
    }
}
```

```text
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci..." /api/v1/balance
--> Balance for rajesh (CUSTOMER): 125000.75 INR
```

### How is token-based auth different from session-based auth?

| Aspect | Token-Based (JWT) | Session-Based |
|---|---|---|
| State | Stateless: token holds identity | Stateful: server stores session |
| Scaling | Any instance validates with the signing key | Needs shared session store or sticky sessions |
| Revocation | Hard: valid until expiry (needs denylist) | Easy: delete session, instant logout |
| Storage | Client holds token | Server holds session record |
| CSRF risk | Low if sent in Authorization header | High because cookie auto-sent |
| Network | No lookup on each request | Lookup on each request |
| Best for | Microservices, SPAs, mobile apps | Server-rendered monoliths |

```java
package com.bank.security;

// Token-based: stateless, no server-side lookup
public class TokenAuth {
    public String identify(String jwt) {
        Claims claims = Jwts.parser().verifyWith(key).build()
            .parseSignedClaims(jwt).getPayload();
        return claims.getSubject(); // "rajesh" -- no DB call
    }
}

// Session-based: stateful, requires server-side lookup
public class SessionAuth {
    public String identify(String sessionId) {
        Object user = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("session:" + sessionId);
        return user != null ? user.toString() : null; // DB/Redis call
    }
}
```

### What is the revocation problem?

Because validation is local and the token is self-contained, you cannot easily revoke a token before it expires. A stolen token is valid until its `exp` claim passes.

The mitigations are short-lived access tokens (about 15 minutes) paired with long-lived refresh tokens, and a server-side denylist of revoked token IDs (`jti`) checked for high-risk actions.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class TokenDenylist {

    // In production, use Redis with TTL matching token expiry
    private static final Set<String> DENIED = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();

    public static void revoke(String jti) {
        DENIED.add(jti);
        System.out.println("Token " + jti + " added to denylist.");
    }

    public static boolean isRevoked(String jti) {
        return DENIED.contains(jti);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        revoke("abc-123-token-id");
        System.out.println("Is revoked: " + isRevoked("abc-123-token-id")); // true
        System.out.println("Is revoked: " + isRevoked("xyz-456-token-id")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
Token abc-123-token-id added to denylist.
Is revoked: true
Is revoked: false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first common mistake is putting sensitive data in the JWT payload. The payload is only Base64Url-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone who captures the token can decode and read every claim.

```java
// WRONG: sensitive data in JWT payload
Jwts.builder()
    .subject("rajesh")
    .claim("accountBalance", "125000.75")  // Leaks financial data!
    .claim("cardNumber", "4532-XXXX-XXXX-1234") // Leaks PII!
    .signWith(key).compact();

// CORRECT: only identifiers and roles
Jwts.builder()
    .subject("rajesh")
    .claim("role", "CUSTOMER")
    .claim("iss", "national-bank")
    .signWith(key).compact();
// Fetch sensitive data from DB after authorization
```

The second common mistake is using long-lived tokens without refresh token rotation, turning a single stolen token into permanent access.

### What about Spring Security integration?

Spring Security provides `BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` that extracts the token from the `Authorization` header and delegates validation to a `JwtDecoder`. Spring Boot auto-configures this when `spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri` is set.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
public class ResourceServerConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/admin/**").hasAuthority("SCOPE_admin")
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/accounts/**").hasAuthority("SCOPE_accounts:read")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt(jwt -> {}));
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that token-based auth and session-based auth are not mutually exclusive. A common pattern is using JWT for inter-service communication in the backend while using session cookies for the browser-facing frontend.

Senior engineers also know that symmetric signing (HMAC) means every service that validates also knows the secret and could forge tokens. Asymmetric signing (RSA or ECDSA) lets the auth server sign with a private key while other services validate with the public key, which is safer in a zero-trust architecture.
